# Copy to `.env` to override the defaults locally. Every variable here is
# read by Vite at BUILD time and baked into the output.
#
# THIS MATTERS FOR DOCKER: the app is built with @sveltejs/adapter-static, so
# what ships is plain HTML, CSS, and JS with no server process behind it.
# There is nothing running at request time that could read an environment
# variable, which means:
#
#   docker build --build-arg VITE_SITE_ORIGIN=https://example.com .   <- works
#   docker run  -e VITE_SITE_ORIGIN=https://example.com  image        <- does nothing
#
# In a Dockerfile that looks like:
#
#   ARG VITE_SITE_ORIGIN=https://app.indienodes.us
#   ENV VITE_SITE_ORIGIN=$VITE_SITE_ORIGIN
#   RUN npm run build
#
# Changing the origin therefore requires a rebuild, not a restart. If the
# origin ever needs to change without one, the value would have to move out
# of the bundle and into something fetched at runtime, which is a different
# design and not what this is.
#
# This file is the complete list, and every variable below has a matching
# --build-arg in the Dockerfile with the same name -- see
# README.md's Docker section for the full docker build invocation. Whether
# any of them are actually set for a given deployment is an infra decision;
# this repo documents what each one does and defaults every optional one to
# its safe, unset behaviour rather than assuming a value. That includes
# VITE_SITE_ORIGIN's own default below: it is a starting point for a bare
# `docker build .` with no args, not this repo's opinion of the "real" value
# -- an infra build that cares what origin ships should always pass its own
# --build-arg rather than rely on it.

# The deployed origin. Used for the absolute Open Graph / Twitter Card URLs
# and, most importantly, by the embeddable widget: it runs on other people's
# sites and so must fetch ring.json with an absolute URL.
# Default: https://app.indienodes.us
VITE_SITE_ORIGIN=https://app.indienodes.us

# Where the submission form on /join posts. Deliberately named for the shape
# rather than the vendor: the contract is "POST JSON, read JSON back", and the
# client neither knows nor cares what is on the other end (an n8n workflow, as
# of this writing).
#
# Leave it unset and the behaviour differs by build, on purpose:
#   npm run dev    -> the form runs against a mock backend, no n8n needed
#   npm run build  -> the form reports that submissions are closed
# The second is the important one. A production build with no backend must not
# look like it is accepting submissions it is quietly dropping.
#
# This URL is PUBLIC. It is compiled into the client bundle, because a static
# site has no server to proxy through. It is not a secret, should not be stored
# as one, and every abuse control (rate limiting, honeypot checks, duplicate
# detection) has to live on the receiving end.
# Default: unset
# VITE_SUBMISSION_WEBHOOK_URL=https://n8n.example.com/webhook/indienodes-submit

# Where the change-request form on /update posts. Deliberately the SAME
# variable as VITE_SUBMISSION_WEBHOOK_URL above, not a separate one: a
# change request needs the exact token-issue/re-verify contract issue_token
# and verify already implement, so it extends that webhook with two more
# `action` values (request_update_token, submit_update) rather than
# standing up a second workflow that would have to reimplement the same
# verification mechanism.

# Where the contact form on /contact posts. A SEPARATE variable from
# VITE_SUBMISSION_WEBHOOK_URL above — Contact is a stateless
# fire-and-forget message with no token/verify contract at all, and keeping
# it on its own webhook means it can be paused or rotated independently of
# the review pipeline. Same rules as VITE_SUBMISSION_WEBHOOK_URL: public,
# unset means dev mocks / prod says the form is closed, no secret-header
# variable exists here either (there is nowhere in this repo to hold one —
# see the note at the top of this file).
# Default: unset
# VITE_CONTACT_WEBHOOK_URL=https://n8n.example.com/webhook/indienodes-contact

# Where the one-time app rating posts. A THIRD webhook, separate from both
# above, for the same reason Contact is separate from submissions: unrelated
# concerns with unrelated failure modes, and switching off rating collection
# should not touch intake or Contact.
#
# It carries the least of the three: a 1-5 integer, a timestamp, the app
# version, and the shared honeypot/dwell pair. No identifier of any kind, and
# nothing to reply to.
#
# Leave it unset and a production build simply never asks for a rating. That is
# quieter than the forms' "closed" state, and correct — nobody is waiting on an
# answer. In dev, unset runs against the mock.
# Default: unset
# VITE_RATING_WEBHOOK_URL=https://n8n.example.com/webhook/indienodes-rating

# Cloudflare Turnstile (CAPTCHA), rendered on /update and /contact. Only the
# site key belongs here: this is a static site with no server of its own
# (see the note at the top of this file), so there is nowhere in this repo
# that could hold the matching secret key and actually call Cloudflare's
# siteverify API. Verification has to happen the same place submission
# validation already does — the external n8n workflow(s) the two webhooks
# above point at — which is where TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY belongs, not here.
# See docs/n8n-workflow-runbook.md for where it's configured.
#
# Create a free Turnstile site at https://dash.cloudflare.com -> Turnstile.
# Leave unset to skip rendering the widget entirely, or use Cloudflare's
# test site key (always passes, no real site needed): 1x00000000000000000000AA
# Default: unset
# VITE_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=

# The About modal's "Support" tab, a Ko-fi donation link. Was a hardcoded
# ko-fi.com/TODO placeholder in src/lib/config.js; now build-time config for
# the same reason as everything else in this file — a personal donation link
# is specific to who's actually running this deployment, not something the
# codebase should assume or leave as a TODO waiting to be caught before
# launch.
# Leave unset to drop the Support tab entirely rather than showing one whose
# link goes nowhere real.
# Default: unset
# VITE_KOFI_URL=https://ko-fi.com/yourhandle

# The repository holding this ring's own members/*.json.
#
# Only read when no submission webhook is configured. In that state /join
# cannot take entries, and with this set it can at least say how to get in
# anyway: open a pull request against members/ in the named repository, which
# build-ring.yml and validate-ring.yml already handle end to end.
#
# Not the same as the codebase's own repository, which is a constant in
# src/lib/config.js — this is the repository *your* ring curates its members
# in, and a fork's is not the upstream one. Unset means the join page simply
# says submissions are not open here, which is better than pointing a
# would-be member at somebody else's ring.
# VITE_RING_REPO_URL=https://github.com/you/your-ring

# Points the app at a different ring.json without touching code.
#
# Two uses, and the second is new. `npm run dev:fixture` sets it to exercise
# the 50-entry fixture in testing/. In a production build it names the
# canonical ring endpoint, for a deployment that should read the live ring
# rather than the copy baked in beside it -- which is what a packaged
# Capacitor or Wails client needs, since its bundled copy is otherwise frozen
# at the moment the app was built.
#
# Accepts `/ring.json`, a full `https://host/path`, or the port-only
# `:4174/ring.test.json` form (resolved against whatever host the page is on,
# so exposing the dev server to a phone needs no reconfiguration).
#
# Unset means `/ring.json` on this origin, which stays the right default for
# the web image: the widget is served from the same origin as the ring, so
# there is one failure domain rather than two.
# Default: /ring.json
# VITE_RING_URL=:4174/ring.test.json

# Whether this deployment presents itself as being in its Early Access /
# founding-ring phase.
#
# The ring opens to the public well before it is dense enough for random
# traversal to be worth much, and those are two different states: the app is
# finished enough to use, the network is not yet finished enough to browse.
# Setting this lets the site say so plainly -- a status chip beside the
# wordmark, a founding-creator pitch on the sparse field, /join and /members,
# and a paragraph in the About modal -- rather than presenting a two-entry
# field as though it were the finished product.
#
# The only boolean in this file, and the only value here whose intended end
# state is unset. Compared against the exact string `true`; anything else,
# `false` included, reads as off, because every VITE_ value arrives as a
# string and a truthy check would read "false" as on. Leaving Early Access is
# therefore removing this variable and rebuilding -- no code change, and every
# Early Access surface comes down together.
#
# Default: unset (not in Early Access)
# VITE_EARLY_ACCESS=true
